            <ArticleTitle>Cocaine abuse and coronary artery dissection.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>340-1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 33-year-old man with a history of recent cocaine use presented with dissection of the left main coronary artery extending distally to involve the left anterior descending (LAD) and circumflex arteries. He required emergency four-vessel aortocoronary bypass, which was uncomplicated.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
